intro.bodyMarkdown:
Lending a book takes two changes: a new row in `loans`, and one fewer copy `available` in `books`. If only the first happened, the numbers would be wrong. A **transaction** groups statements so they happen **all together or not at all**:

```
BEGIN;
INSERT INTO loans (member_id, book_id, borrowed, due)
VALUES (7, 4, '2026-10-24', '2026-11-07');
UPDATE books SET available = available - 1 WHERE id = 4;
COMMIT;
```

`BEGIN` starts the transaction. Until `COMMIT`, the changes are only provisional: the database keeps the old version safe, and if anything goes wrong, nothing is half done. `COMMIT` makes them permanent.

`ROLLBACK` throws the changes away instead, and the data is back to how it was at `BEGIN`:

```
BEGIN;
DELETE FROM loans;
SELECT COUNT(*) AS during FROM loans;
ROLLBACK;
SELECT COUNT(*) AS after FROM loans;
```

That makes a transaction a safe way to try something: make the change, look at the result, then `COMMIT` if it is right or `ROLLBACK` if not. A `SAVEPOINT name` inside a transaction marks a point you can go back to with `ROLLBACK TO name`, without undoing the whole transaction.

Without `BEGIN`, SQLite treats each statement as its own little transaction and saves it straight away. `COMMIT` or `ROLLBACK` with no `BEGIN` before it gives the error **no transaction is active**.

